<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet">
<meta name="googlebot" content="noindex, nofollow">
<title>Assessment · Merkle Analytic Platform Assessment</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700;800&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="css/app.css">
<script src="config.js"></script>
</head>
<body data-page="assessment">
<header class="brandbar"><div class="brandbar-inner">
  <a href="/dashboard" class="mk-logo"><span class="mk-logo-img" role="img" aria-label="Merkle"></span><span class="mk-print-word">Merkle</span></a>
  <span class="mk-sep"></span><span class="mk-title">Analytic Platform Assessment</span>
</div></header>
<div class="subnav">
  <a class="backlink" href="/dashboard">← Dashboard</a>
  <span class="sn-title" id="snTitle">Assessment</span>
  <span class="autosave" id="autosave"><span class="dot"></span><span id="autosaveText">—</span></span>
</div>
<div class="wrap">
  <nav class="tabs">
    <button class="tab active" data-tab="usecases">1 · Use Cases</button>
    <button class="tab" data-tab="discovery">2 · Prioritization</button>
    <button class="tab" data-tab="results">3 · Results</button>
  </nav>
  <section class="panel active" data-panel="usecases">
    <div class="readme">
      <h4>Capturing analytical use cases</h4>
      <p>Use cases are the <strong>business outcomes</strong> the client wants analytics to deliver — populated ahead of the workshop from what you already know, then refined live with the client. Pick from the common library, edit any use case to match the client's exact situation, and map each to the capabilities that would deliver it. <strong>Capabilities without any linked use case have no client demand behind them</strong>, and are candidates to downgrade in the next tab.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-library-block">
      <div class="uc-block-head"><span class="uc-block-title">Common use cases</span><span class="uc-block-hint">Click to add — you can edit anything after</span></div>
      <div id="ucLibrary" class="uc-library"></div>
      <div class="uc-custom-row"><button class="btn" id="btnAddCustomUC">+ Add custom use case</button></div>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-selected-block">
      <div class="uc-block-head"><span class="uc-block-title">In scope for this client</span><span class="uc-block-hint" id="ucCount">0 use cases</span></div>
      <div id="ucSelected" class="uc-selected"></div>
      <div id="ucEmpty" class="uc-empty">No use cases captured yet. Add one from the library above, or draft a custom one for this client.</div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="panel" data-panel="discovery">
    <div class="readme">
      <h4>Setting your priorities</h4>
      <p>Apply a <strong>MoSCoW priority</strong> to each capability — <em>Must</em>, <em>Should</em>, <em>Could</em>, or <em>Won't</em> (out of scope). The use cases you captured on the previous tab appear under each capability as a working prompt: <strong>a capability with no linked use case has nothing on the client's list riding on it</strong> and is a candidate for <em>Should</em>, <em>Could</em>, or <em>Won't</em>. Open a capability to fine-tune which sub-capabilities are in play.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="framework"></div>
  </section>
  <section class="panel" data-panel="results">
    <div class="readme">
      <h4>Reading your results</h4>
      <p>Weighted fit is the MoSCoW-weighted average of capability coverage. A <strong>Must</strong> capability with zero coverage is a knockout — it flags the platform rather than merely costing points. The <strong>Coverage by use case</strong> section below re-cuts the same data around the outcomes captured on the Use Cases tab, and names the platform best positioned to deliver them. Profiles at the bottom are written against <em>your</em> priorities: strengths are the priorities a platform fully meets, and gaps call out partial or disqualifying coverage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="results-actions"><button class="btn" id="btnPrint">Print / PDF</button></div>
    <h3 class="sec-h">Weighted fit &amp; recommended stack</h3>
    <div class="scoreboard">
      <div id="rankList" class="rank-list"></div>
      <div id="stackCard" class="stack-card"></div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="sec-h" id="ucSecHead" hidden>Coverage by use case</h3>
    <div id="ucCoverage" class="uc-coverage-wrap" hidden></div>
    <h3 class="sec-h">Coverage by pillar</h3>
    <div class="pill-grid"><table class="agg" id="aggTable"></table></div>
    <h3 class="sec-h">Platform profiles</h3>
    <div id="profiles" class="profiles"></div>
    <p class="footnote">Methodology: capability coverage = supported ÷ in-scope sub-capabilities per capability; weighted fit averages those by MoSCoW weight (Must 3 · Should 2 · Could 1). Use case coverage aggregates the same sub-capability support across the capabilities each use case is mapped to, respecting scope (Won't capabilities are excluded). Best-for-use-cases ranks by number of use cases fully delivered, then average coverage. Ratings reflect documented platform capability and Merkle SME judgement, not vendor marketing.</p>
  </section>
</div>
<footer class="site-foot">
  <div class="foot-inner">
    <div class="foot-brand"><span class="mk-logo"><span class="mk-logo-img" role="img" aria-label="Merkle"></span><span class="mk-print-word">Merkle</span></span><div class="foot-tag">Analytic Platform Assessment</div></div>
    <div class="foot-method">
      <h3>About this assessment &amp; methodology</h3>
      <p>This assessment reflects Merkle's analytics consulting practice &mdash; a structured, vendor-neutral way to match analytics needs to the platforms in market, refined across enterprise platform selections. It is not a feature checklist: every judgement in the underlying rubric encodes our consultants' hands-on experience of where each platform genuinely performs or falls short in production.</p>
      <p>The method is deliberately simple and defensible. Capabilities are grouped into pillars. You assign a MoSCoW priority to each capability &mdash; <b>Must</b>, <b>Should</b>, <b>Could</b> or <b>Won't</b> &mdash; to express how important it is. Each platform's coverage of a capability is measured from a yes/no assessment of its sub-capabilities, maintained in our master rubric, and rolls up to a priority-weighted fit score (Must&nbsp;3, Should&nbsp;2, Could&nbsp;1). A Must a platform cannot meet at all is treated as a disqualifier rather than a deduction, so a strong all-rounder never outranks a platform that clears every non-negotiable. The output is a best-of-breed recommendation: a lead platform, plus any specialist platforms that close priority gaps.</p>
      <p class="foot-fine">Assessments represent Merkle's point of view and are validated against current vendor releases at the time of engagement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>
<script src="js/core.js"></script>
<script type="module" src="js/db.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
